--- machine\generic\authn\policy
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy

What this machine grants a principal. One subkey per principal, named by
the principal; the values in it are that principal's whole authority.

Keyed by principal rather than by privilege so that **one key shows the
totality of what somebody may do**. Authority scattered across twenty
values is authority nobody audits: a privilege granted somewhere
unexpected does not surface when you look at the principal, and you would
have to know to check every other place.

Under `Generic` rather than `Software\Authd` because this is Peios policy
about what a logon means, not configuration belonging to one authority's
implementation. Another authority would read the same key.

**If the key exists, the key is the whole answer.** There is no per-value
merge with a compiled table. Either the key is absent — an unconfigured
machine, which falls back to a compiled floor so it still boots and still
logs in — or the key exists and nothing compiled contributes anything to
privileges.

That rule is deliberate, and the alternative was rejected on security
grounds: an administrator who writes `Administrators` = `SeBackup`
believing they have locked the machine down would, under a merge, still be
handing out whatever a table they never saw said for every principal they
did not mention. A partial policy instead makes things stop working, which
is an availability failure — worse to hit, far better to diagnose.

The floor grants `SeChangeNotifyPrivilege` to `Everyone` and nothing else:
without it a process cannot traverse a directory to reach a file, so a
machine whose policy was never seeded still starts a shell, with no
administrative power whatsoever. On any seeded machine the key exists, so
the floor is inert by construction.

**Present but unreadable is not absent.** A descriptor that locked authd
out, or a damaged hive, grants nothing rather than falling back to the
floor — treating it as absent would silently restore every privilege an
administrator had deliberately removed, at the one moment nobody can
check.

authd reads at most 256 records and warns past that; it is a bound on work
per logon, not a policy limit.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and
Administrators full, Authenticated Users read). That descriptor is the
only gate on changing what this machine grants.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy deniedprivileges
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy DeniedPrivileges
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: privilege names; an unknown name is dropped with a warning
applies: next logon

Privileges no principal may hold, whatever any record says.

The lockdown knob. Privileges union across every SID on a token, so
without this, denying something means finding and editing every record
that grants it. This is applied **last**, after the union, so it is one
edit that no record you have not read can defeat.

It sits on the parent key rather than in a record so it cannot collide
with a principal who happens to be called `Denied`.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy logonsocketdescriptor
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy LogonSocketDescriptor
type: REG_SZ
default: O:SYG:SYD:P(A;;GA;;;SY)(A;;GA;;;BA)
valid: SDDL; a malformed value falls back to the built-in
applies: restart

Who may reach `/run/logon.sock`, as SDDL.

The outer gate on originating a logon — asking authd to authenticate
somebody else. The compiled default admits SYSTEM (which is what the
built-in `login` runs as) and Administrators. Widening it is genuinely
site policy: a graphical greeter, a web console, a remote access daemon.

**Widening the socket alone changes nothing.** The descriptor decides who
may connect; `LogonTypes` on that peer's own record decides what they may
then ask for, and a peer with no record may originate nothing. Both edits
are needed, which is the point — the socket is not the grant.

Read once at start and applied when the socket is bound, so a change needs
a restart. Rereading it per logon would only misreport what is actually in
force on the socket.

authd's other two sockets are not configurable here. `/run/psi.sock` is
authd's own protocol and its requirement is authd's to state;
`/run/ident.sock` admits everyone a connect needs, because a service
account that cannot resolve its own name is worse than useless. All three
are protected, so `/run`'s inheritance can neither widen nor narrow them.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal>
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal>

One principal's authority. The key name is the principal, and every value
in it is optional — absent means "this record does not speak to that",
which is not the same as "none".

The name is a bare well-known name — `Everyone`, `Administrators`,
`Users`, `SYSTEM`, `Local Service`, `Network Service`, `Authenticated
Users`, `Guests`, `Anonymous`, `Local`, `Domain Admins`, or a logon-type
name (`Interactive`, `Network`, `Batch`, `Service`) — or a literal SID.
Matching is case-insensitive. `BUILTIN\Administrators` is unrepresentable,
because a backslash is the registry's path separator.

A name that is neither is **ignored with a warning** rather than silently
applying to nobody. Resolution happens once, up front, precisely so a
mistyped principal is visible instead of sitting in the key looking
authoritative forever.

A record applies when the token carries that SID — as the user or as any
group, including the ones authd derives: `Everyone`, `Authenticated
Users`, `Local`, and the logon-type SID. So policy can be written against
how a logon happened, not only who it was.

**How records compose differs per value**, because the values are
different kinds of thing:

- `Privileges` is a set, so it **unions** across every applicable record.
- `Integrity` is a single number that has to be chosen: the user's own
  record wins outright, otherwise the maximum across their groups.
- `Owner` and `DefaultDacl` are single values with no ordering: the user's
  own record wins, otherwise a group may name one, and two groups
  disagreeing falls back rather than picking arbitrarily.
- `LogonTypes` is consulted with the peer's user SID **alone** — no group
  union at all.

Precedence is per value, not per record. A user record that sets
`Privileges` but no `Integrity` does not suppress the group computation
for integrity; it simply does not speak to it.

A service's own record is how a daemon gets authority nothing else has: a
service token carries an `S-1-5-80` SID derived from the service name, so
a record named for it grants that service alone rather than everything
running as `LocalService`.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> privileges
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> Privileges
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent — this record grants no privileges)
valid: Se...Privilege names; unknown names dropped with a warning
applies: next logon

The privileges this principal is granted, present and enabled on the
token.

Enabled, not merely present: a privilege a caller had to switch on before
it worked would be a grant in name only.

These **union** across every record the token matches, then
`DeniedPrivileges` is subtracted. An empty list grants nothing; an absent
value means this record does not speak to privileges.

An unknown name is dropped with a warning rather than failing the whole
list. The names are an ABI vocabulary that grows, so a policy written for
a newer Peios can legitimately mention a privilege this build cannot name,
and refusing the whole record would take away the privileges the
administrator did spell correctly. Dropping one grants strictly less,
which is the safe direction for a grant — but it also means a typo costs
you that privilege silently, so check the log after an edit.

`SeCreateTokenPrivilege`, `SeTcbPrivilege` and
`SeAssignPrimaryTokenPrivilege` belong to the TCB and should never appear
here; granting them makes every other control on this key decorative.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> integrity
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> Integrity
type: REG_SZ or REG_DWORD
default: Medium (compiled in unconditionally)
valid: Untrusted, Low, Medium, High, System — or any DWORD
applies: next logon

The integrity level this principal's token carries.

Both spellings are accepted because they do different jobs: the tier name
is the readable form of the common case, and a raw number reaches levels
no name can express, since the kernel compares integrity numerically and
any value is legal. Names match case-insensitively; an unrecognised name
warns and falls back to the default.

Unlike privileges, the default is compiled in **unconditionally** rather
than coming from the floor — a level is not a grant, and every token must
carry one to be valid, so "the key exists" cannot be allowed to mean "mint
at Untrusted".

The composition rule has a consequence worth knowing in advance: **a group
cannot impose an integrity floor on a member.** The user's own record wins
outright, so if `Administrators` names High and a member's own record
names Low, that member gets Low. That is what makes lowering a single
principal possible at all without a second mechanism.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> owner
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> Owner
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — the principal owns what they create)
valid: a well-known name or a SID; unresolvable warns and is ignored
applies: next logon

Which principal owns the objects this token creates.

The case it exists for is "objects created by administrators are owned by
`Administrators`" rather than by the individual, which is why a group
record may name one.

Named rather than indexed, deliberately: the token field is an index into
the token's own SID array, a number only authd can compute and one that
would mean something different on the next logon.

Two groups naming **different** owners is a misconfiguration, and it falls
back to the principal themselves with a warning rather than picking one —
group records have no order, and registry enumeration order is not
something policy should depend on. Two naming the same owner is not a
conflict.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> defaultdacl
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> DefaultDacl
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — the kernel's own default applies)
valid: SDDL; a malformed value warns and is ignored
applies: next logon

The DACL objects this token creates inherit when nothing else supplies
one.

SDDL rather than raw bytes because this has to be **auditable**. The whole
argument for policy living in the registry is that an operator can read
it, and a binary ACL in a policy key is exactly the thing nobody checks.

One trap, and it is the reason the shipped seed looks the way it does: the
kernel copies a default DACL onto a new object **verbatim**, so a
`CREATOR OWNER` placeholder is never substituted and would name nobody.
Grant the owner through `OWNER RIGHTS` (`S-1-3-4`) instead. The seed ships
`D:(A;;GA;;;S-1-3-4)(A;;GA;;;SY)(A;;GA;;;BA)` on `Everyone` for exactly
this reason.

A malformed value is dropped rather than failing the logon, so a typo
costs the customisation and not the session — but the result is a token
with no default DACL, and an object it creates with no parent to inherit
from then gets a **null** DACL. Check the log after editing this one.

Same precedence as `Owner`: the user's own record wins, otherwise a group
may name one, and two groups disagreeing falls back to none.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> logontypes
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> LogonTypes
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent — this principal may originate nothing)
valid: six logon-type names (below); unknown names dropped with a warning
applies: next logon

The logon types this principal may **originate** — ask authd to perform on
behalf of somebody else, over `/run/logon.sock`.

Valid names in full: `Interactive`, `Network`, `Batch`, `Service`,
`NetworkCleartext`, `NewCredentials`. An unknown name is dropped with a
warning, on the same reasoning as `Privileges`.

The other half of "client proposes, authority constrains". This is the
machine's statement about the **asker**, distinct from the per-principal
logon types a source asserts about the account being signed in. A
graphical greeter gets `Interactive`, a web console `Network`, and neither
can mint itself a console-shaped token by proposing a different type.

Consulted with the socket peer's user SID and **nothing else** — no group
union. Origination is checked before any token exists, so there is no
final SID set to evaluate against, and a right this consequential should
be granted by name rather than assembled from memberships nobody stated
together.

SYSTEM may originate anything without a record. For everybody else, no
record or no value means **nothing**: an absent value and an explicitly
empty list are both refusals here, and the distinction that matters
elsewhere does not apply.

--- machine\software\authd
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd

authd's own configuration, as distinct from the policy it enforces.

One subkey today: `Sources`, the allowlist of principal sources. What a
logon means — privileges, integrity, who may originate one — is not
here; it lives under `Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy`, because that is Peios
policy any authority would read rather than authd's implementation.

--- machine\software\authd\sources
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources

Which principal sources may register with authd, and what each is trusted
to say. One subkey per source.

**A subkey's name is the allowlist entry.** `Sources\lpsd` says "a service
named lpsd may register as a principal source", and authd checks that by
deriving lpsd's service SID and requiring the connecting peer's token to
carry it. So `reg ls` on this key answers "what may assert identity on
this machine?" exactly, with no second list anywhere to drift out of step.

**An empty or absent key means no source may register.** An absent
policy value means "use the documented default"; an absent allowlist
must mean "nobody", because an allowlist that fails open is not an
allowlist. The cost is that an image shipping no seed cannot authenticate
anyone — which is the correct way for that mistake to present.

authd considers at most 64 entries and warns past that; it is a bound on
work done for an unauthenticated connection, not a policy limit.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and
Administrators full, Authenticated Users read). Write access here is the
power to decide who may vouch for identity on this machine.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source>
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source>

One principal source. The key name is the service name, and it is the
allowlist entry itself — the values inside only narrow what that source
may then say.

authd derives the service SID from this name and requires the connecting
peer to carry it. The name is taken from the registry and never from the
connecting process, so a source proves nothing by asserting it: peinit
already did, by minting the token. A name whose service SID cannot be
derived is ignored with a warning.

Every value below is optional. The standard local source, seeded by lpsd's
package as `Sources\lpsd`, sets `MayAssertForeignMemberships`,
`UnixIDBase` and `UnixIDCount`.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> searchorder
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> SearchOrder
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1000
valid: any; lower is consulted first
applies: next registration

Where this source sits when authd resolves a name nobody qualified.

This is the value that decides who `jack` is on a machine with more than
one source. It is deliberately explicit: resolving in registration order
would let a slow disk change which principal a name refers to, and two
components disagreeing about that is a confused deputy rather than a
cosmetic inconsistency.

Sources sharing a value are ordered by name, so the result never depends
on which registered first. The default is mid-range so a source can be
configured either side of it without renumbering the ones already there.

A value that is not a REG_DWORD warns and resolves at the default.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> domain
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> Domain
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — unpinned, which is not unrestricted)
valid: a SID; malformed means this source cannot register
applies: next registration

Pins the domain this source is permitted to declare.

A source declares the domain it is authoritative for when it registers,
and authd confines every identity it asserts to that domain. The
declaration is a claim — a source generates its own domain, so nothing
about the SID can prove the claim honest — and this value is how a site
states what the answer must be.

Absent means unpinned, **not** unrestricted: authd still requires the
domain to be a claimable shape, to not collide with another registered
source's, and to be the same on re-registration.

A malformed value is deliberately not treated as absent. Absence means "no
pin"; an unparseable value means an administrator tried to write one and
got it wrong, and quietly downgrading that to "unpinned" would remove the
control they were applying, silently, at the moment they applied it.
Instead the source is pinned to the Null SID, which nothing can satisfy,
so it simply cannot register.

**authd never writes this.** Pinning on first registration would need the
process holding `SeCreateTokenPrivilege` to hold a registry write handle;
having the source write it would be the lower-trust party writing into the
key that governs what it may do.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> unixidbase
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> UnixIDBase
type: REG_DWORD
default: (absent — no range, so principals project to nobody)
valid: 1000000 or above; below that is refused
applies: next registration

The bottom of this source's Unix ID range.

authd adds it to every relative id the source asserts, so with a base of
1,000,000 the source's first principal — number 1 — projects to uid
1,000,001. A source never learns its own base, so rebasing is a registry
edit rather than a rewrite of every record the source holds.

Everything below 1,000,000 belongs to authd: the well-known SIDs, and the
service and confinement SIDs numbered out of that band. A base below it is
refused, which is the numeric half of the same confinement as the domain
pin — it is why a source cannot reach **uid 0**.

**Absent means no range, and no range means `nobody`.** Every principal
from an unconfigured source projects to 65534. That is the safe direction:
65534 grants nothing, where assuming a base would put two sources on the
same numbers the moment a second one existed.

A malformed or unusable value is treated as absent rather than as a
refusal — a range is a grant, so discarding it grants strictly less.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> unixidcount
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> UnixIDCount
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1000000
valid: 1 or more; base + count must not overflow
applies: next registration

How many ids this source's range spans.

Not decoration: a source asserts relative numbers, so without a ceiling
a large enough one would land inside the next source's range. A relative
id at or past the count is refused rather than wrapped or clamped —
clamping would silently put two principals on one number.

A value that is not a REG_DWORD warns and the default is used. A range
that is unusable as a whole (empty, or overflowing) is treated as no range
at all, so the source's principals project to `nobody`.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> mayassertforeignmemberships
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> MayAssertForeignMemberships
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: non-zero to set; absent or 0 is off
applies: next registration

Lifts the cross-reference restriction on the group memberships this
source may assert.

An ordinary source may only assert groups in the same domain as the
principal it just authenticated: a directory vouches for its own users and
its own groups, and nothing else. The local source is different in kind —
it is authoritative for local group membership of any principal,
including domain ones, which is what makes "`CORP\Domain Admins` is in
`BUILTIN\Administrators`" a local record rather than something a domain
controller can assert at you.

**Memberships only, never identity.** A source with this set still may not
be the authority on who someone is outside its own domain, and the
distinction is not academic: the local source holds no domain credential,
so an identity-unrestricted local source could hand a domain identity to
anyone who knew a local password. Confining identity keeps a compromise
at "administrator of this machine" rather than "any principal in the
forest".

This grants a permission rather than restoring a default, so silence means
no: absent, zero, or a value that is not a REG_DWORD all leave it off.
